From 16899e2a7e6cb89bed6ca9d0d1e0df8839961c97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yzddmr6 <46088090+yzddmr6@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 20:19:04 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] drm/msm/dpu: restore the core clock OPP vote before
 enabling clocks on resume

When the last CRTC is disabled, dpu_core_perf_crtc_update() computes a
core clock rate of 0 and passes it to dev_pm_opp_set_rate(), which
disables the OPP table: the bandwidth and required-opps votes go to 0
while the clock keeps its rate. The DPU then runtime suspends and genpd
saves that performance state of 0; genpd_runtime_resume() restores it
before dpu_runtime_resume() runs. dpu_runtime_resume() enables the core
clock straight away, at the rate it had before, while the DPU has no
vote on its power domain. The vote only comes back with the first
dpu_core_perf_crtc_update() from atomic_flush, after the encoders and
the DSI hosts have already been brought up with the MDP clock running.

On SM8750 the core clock stays at 575 MHz, which needs nom_l1 on MMCX,
while the only remaining vote on MMCX is the display clock controller's
low_svs. On the OnePlus Pad 2 Pro, once rpmhpd reaches sync_state and
actually lowers MMCX, the first unblank after a blank hangs the whole
SoC: bounded on-device forensics show the DPU voting 320 (nom_l1)
before the blank, 0 while blanked, and the board going silent within
a second of the unblank (the last netconsole line comes about 20 ms
after it), with or without the MDSS GDSC collapsing in between. Before
rpmhpd reached sync_state every rail was held at its highest level,
which hid the window.

Vote for the rate the core clock is about to run at, capped at the
highest OPP, before enabling the clocks. This mirrors the DSI host,
which sets its OPP before enabling the link clocks. The resume done
from dpu_kms_hw_init() comes before the perf limits are known and is
skipped; dpu_kms_init() has voted for the highest OPP by then, and
genpd restores that vote on resume. Without an OPP table
dev_pm_opp_set_rate() reduces to setting the clock to the rate it
already has.

The suspend side needs no change: disabling the CRTC has already
dropped the vote, and genpd drops whatever the device still votes when
it runtime suspends and restores it on the next resume. Once the vote
is restored here it follows the same save and restore until the next
CRTC disable sets it to 0 again, and the next CRTC update adjusts it as
before.

(cherry picked from commit 3a56ea9421bb21d00a3247ed12b74c2f1fd74b10)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Origin: liuqin kernel commit 8ec7416f90f6, taken as it is. The cherry-pick
line above names 3a56ea9421bb, on a topic branch that did not reach mainline.
It was written for SM8750, where the first unblank after rpmhpd settles hangs
the SoC; on this device (SM8475) the same window exists and nothing has hung
in it yet.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c | 42 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 42 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c
index da3556e..0982812 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c
@@ -1446,6 +1446,44 @@ static int __maybe_unused dpu_runtime_suspend(struct device *dev)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Disabling the last CRTC drops the core clock's performance vote through
+ * dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev, 0), and the power domain restores exactly that
+ * on runtime resume. The clock keeps its rate though, and runs at it as
+ * soon as it is enabled below, well before the first CRTC update votes
+ * again. Put the vote for that rate back before the clock is enabled.
+ */
+static int dpu_kms_restore_core_clk_vote(struct dpu_kms *dpu_kms)
+{
+	struct clk *core_clk;
+	unsigned long rate;
+	int ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * The resume in dpu_kms_hw_init() comes before the perf limits are
+	 * known, but after dpu_kms_init() voted for the highest OPP.
+	 */
+	if (!dpu_kms->perf.max_core_clk_rate)
+		return 0;
+
+	core_clk = msm_clk_bulk_get_clock(dpu_kms->clocks, dpu_kms->num_clocks,
+					  "core");
+	if (!core_clk)
+		return 0;
+
+	rate = clk_get_rate(core_clk);
+	if (!rate)
+		return 0;
+
+	rate = min_t(unsigned long, rate, dpu_kms->perf.max_core_clk_rate);
+	ret = dev_pm_opp_set_rate(&dpu_kms->pdev->dev, rate);
+	if (ret)
+		DPU_ERROR("failed to vote for core clock rate %lu: %d\n",
+			  rate, ret);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
 static int __maybe_unused dpu_runtime_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	int rc = -1;
@@ -1457,6 +1495,10 @@ static int __maybe_unused dpu_runtime_resume(struct device *dev)
 
 	ddev = dpu_kms->dev;
 
+	rc = dpu_kms_restore_core_clk_vote(dpu_kms);
+	if (rc)
+		return rc;
+
 	rc = clk_bulk_prepare_enable(dpu_kms->num_clocks, dpu_kms->clocks);
 	if (rc) {
 		DPU_ERROR("clock enable failed rc:%d\n", rc);
-- 
